          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fasten off, leaving a 15 cm tail. Thread through a tapestry needle and weave through 3 to 4 stitches on the back of round 6, then reverse direction through 2 to 3 stitches. Trim close. Weave in the magic ring tail along the back of round 1. Steam-block on a blocking mat: pin the four corners first, then ease the four sides straight and pin at each cluster before applying steam."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Joining" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The six-round square joins at its four corner ch-2 spaces and its 20 side ch-1 spaces. For a 40 x 40 cm cushion cover front, lay out nine squares in a 3 x 3 grid and join with a flat slip-stitch seam on the wrong side, matching corner spaces corner-to-corner and side spaces along the joining edges."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Care" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash in cool water with mild soap, or machine-wash on a delicates cycle. Do not tumble-dry. Lay flat and ease to shape while still damp."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": { "level": 2 },
        "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "What to try next" }]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The eight-round version reaches 20 cm and works as a standalone pot holder or small panel. For a version with a different texture at the border, try the granny square with a shell edging on the final round instead of the standard cluster border."
          }
        ]
      }
